    "nodes": [
      {
        "citable": true,
        "decl_name": "encard_image_restrict_le_encard_pairCubes",
        "depth": 0,
        "dictionary_leaves": [
          "pairCubes"
        ],
        "doc": "**Pajor's inequality for multiclass concept classes**, with no hypotheses on the domain, the\nlabel type or the family: the traces of `𝒞` on `S` are at most as many as the pair-cubes of `𝒞`\nsupported inside `S`.\n\nThe count runs on a split of the family at a point where two traces differ, taking one branch\nper realised value with no residual bucket. A cube of a branch never constrains the split point,\nso a cube pair-shattered by `μ` branches is counted `μ` times on the left and supplies\n`1 + μ.choose 2` cubes on the right, which closes the accounting because `μ ≤ 1 + μ.choose 2`\nfor `μ ≥ 1`, with equality exactly at `μ ∈ {1, 2}`. An infinite trace family forces infinitely\nmany one-point cubes and both sides are `⊤`.\n\nAt `Y = Bool` this specialises to `encard_image_inter_le_encard_shatters`, which is\n`encard_image_inter_le_encard_shatters_of_multiclass` below. The pair-cube is Natarajan's\nshattering witness for spaces of functions, from p. 81 of *On learning sets and functions*; see\nthe module docstring.",
        "is_root": true,
        "kind": "theorem",
        "pretty": "∀ {X : Type u_1} {Y : Type u_2} (𝒞 : Set (X → Y)) (S : Set X), (S.restrict '' 𝒞).encard ≤ (pairCubes 𝒞 S).encard",
        "topo": 0,
        "type_sha256": "ee80b2b451a97d647500c937822715ba027e42463fd164000994237172d476d8"
      },
      {
        "citable": true,
        "decl_name": "infinite_pairCubes_of_infinite_image_restrict",
        "depth": 1,
        "dictionary_leaves": [
          "pairCubes",
          "pairSupport",
          "singCube"
        ],
        "doc": "**Infinitely many traces force infinitely many cubes.** Either the family disagrees at\ninfinitely many points of `S`, each of which carries a one-point cube, or it realises infinitely\nmany labels at one point, which carries infinitely many one-point cubes. The proof runs the\ncontrapositive: finitely many cubes bound both the disagreement set and, above each of its\npoints, the set of realised labels, and a trace is determined by its values on the disagreement\nset, so the traces embed in a finite product. This is what makes the multiclass inequality\nhypothesis-free, as `infinite_setOf_shatters` does in the binary case.",
        "hypotheses": [
          {
            "name": "h",
            "pretty": "(S.restrict '' 𝒞).Infinite"
          }
        ],
        "is_root": false,
        "kind": "theorem",
        "pretty": "∀ {X : Type u_1} {Y : Type u_2} {𝒞 : Set (X → Y)} {S : Set X}, (S.restrict '' 𝒞).Infinite → (pairCubes 𝒞 S).Infinite",
        "topo": 1,
        "type_sha256": "7141fdb9aeb5035019e84282a733833905de91afee081d51d8e8d43bb4aacdbd"
      },
      {
        "citable": false,
        "decl_name": "singCube_right_injective",
        "depth": 2,
        "dictionary_leaves": [
          "pairOf",
          "singCube"
        ],
        "is_root": false,
        "kind": "theorem",
        "pretty": "∀ {X : Type u_1} {Y : Type u_2} (z : X) (u : Y), Function.Injective (singCube✝ z u)",
        "topo": 2,
        "type_sha256": "1d55a4dbe5711ee7924d9125abd20c82b147a95ab09ecb5d5aa9f5fd2108874c"
      },
